WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.950
- Wow, I'm blown away. I'm gobsmacked.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.690
First, I would like to
thank the organizers

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.592
for inviting me to speak
at this lovely event.

00:00:08.592 --> 00:00:11.910
(audience laughs)

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.410
I had nothing to do with that.

00:00:15.332 --> 00:00:16.980
This has been a wonderful meeting.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.920
I'm gonna try and not mess
it up here at the end.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.760
So everyone's been showing pictures

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.293
of the birthday boy.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.363
Here's the oldest baby picture
I could find of circuit QED.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.930
So as Leo mentioned,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:34.950
and many of you know

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.481
we have a kind of tradition in the group,

00:00:37.481 --> 00:00:39.270
which has kind of fallen by the wayside

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.530
with PowerPoint and everything,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.050
but, like, people would bring a printout

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.360
and we'd date it and sign
it and things like that.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.790
And so this is a printout

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.220
that I think Andreas put on my door.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.140
It's a phase of a dispersive
measurement versus gate charge.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.660
And he just put the two, you
know, double question marks,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.560
like he wasn't even sure.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.870
It's just really, but it's
periodic and gate charge.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.180
So it may be like we're
finding the Cooper pair box

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.160
with this dispersive measurement.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.447
And a lot of people have
also been asking me,

00:01:07.447 --> 00:01:09.630
"Is your birthday today?"

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.384
My birthday is actually the 24th.

00:01:11.384 --> 00:01:13.470
And you can notice here,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.876
this apparently occurred on the 25th.

00:01:15.876 --> 00:01:16.950
(audience laughs)

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.830
So this was Andreas bringing
me a 40th birthday present.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.192
So for all the people I'm
currently working with,

00:01:22.192 --> 00:01:24.011
what are you bringing me for my birthday?

00:01:24.011 --> 00:01:27.928
(audience laughs and applauds)

00:01:30.943 --> 00:01:33.123
Bring yourselves. That'll be enough.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.830
And, you know, it was an amazing time.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.720
Things went rather quickly.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.360
We had the dispersive
measurement by the March meeting,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.893
like, in beautiful form.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.930
We came back and actually my
recollection is Andreas really

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.400
wanted to get the vacuum Rabi splitting

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.690
by tuning everything into resonance.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.450
I said "That's too hard.
Let's just write this up."

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:55.560
I give you like two weeks

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.957
after the March meeting to
get the vacuum Rabi splitting.

00:01:57.957 --> 00:02:00.750
And so here it is, I guess this was April,

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.640
maybe we had it working already.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.330
This is already like the draft
of the figure for the paper

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.850
and we mailed it off.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.820
I think the first person we showed this to

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.360
is I sent it off to Jeff Kimball,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.420
who had been my instructor
for applications

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.507
of quantum physics back at Caltech.

00:02:17.507 --> 00:02:20.160
And I'm not sure he ever forgave me

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.630
because he was the first

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.418
to observe this in the atomic
domain and it was really hard

00:02:24.418 --> 00:02:27.630
and they never quite got
to as much cooperativity

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:29.193
and all that as these things.

00:02:31.207 --> 00:02:33.300
It was my first "Nature" paper.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.630
We didn't get on the cover of "Nature."

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.020
I don't know if they still do this.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:40.505
They invite you to submit
some candidate cover art

00:02:40.505 --> 00:02:43.380
and apparently the mistake
we made was letting Dave

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:44.424
draft the cover art.

00:02:44.424 --> 00:02:45.870
(audience laughing)

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.200
And so you see, you can read it here,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.280
maybe it's faint.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.940
Biology NIH decides to
spend money on physics.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.480
Chemistry gives up in the
face of bio and the physics.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.622
And so if anyone who is making
the decision was not like

00:03:00.622 --> 00:03:03.993
a quantum physicist, we were
clearly gonna get posed.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:10.440
But I wanna say thank you.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.060
Here's the picture of
sort of the circuit QED

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.660
core back in the day

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.150
and it was a wonderful time as
you know, a privilege to work

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.740
with such special people.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.290
And you know, not only

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.309
'cause it was early in the lab
doing experiments and things

00:03:28.309 --> 00:03:30.930
and we were still feeling our way along

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.960
but also 'cause we were
learning this language

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.930
of quantum optics and how
to apply it to circuits.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.820
And I remember when we posted the paper,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.747
many of the people in the field said,

00:03:40.747 --> 00:03:44.460
"Think that was a great paper
but what's a Purcell effect

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.050
and why is there a vacuum Rabi?"

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.720
And like you know, took
a while for people.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.930
Now everyone knows this lingo,

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.060
and I also want to say thank you to

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.243
my partners in crime
for the last 20 years,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.300
Steve and Michel.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.220
I mean what a privilege to work with two

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.140
of the best scientists on the planet

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.900
for all these 20 years
in close collaboration.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.570
And I don't think that's something that

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.650
you're supposed to expect when you go

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.213
into this as a line of work.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.210
And there's been incredible progress.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:25.650
We've heard a lot about it in
this meeting in the 20 years.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.380
You know, we've gone from
nanoseconds to milliseconds.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.330
That's called something, I forget what.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.650
Readout fidelities have gone up.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.310
We've heard, you know, really
amazing numbers even advancing

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.480
in the presentations in
this meeting complexity.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.373
I guess we have to add a zero Jerry,

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.743
a thousand qubits.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.440
Size, we went from like a
two by seven millimeter chip

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.173
to 30 millimeters as Andreas told us.

00:04:55.173 --> 00:04:57.600
UN told us that we've gone from zero mass

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.250
to I think it was 16 micrograms.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.950
Maybe there's other numbers
that are bigger than that.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.493
The funding, I don't know.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.980
And I was only thinking
about the academic funding.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.470
Let's not even think about
the private sector funding

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:17.580
that goes into it, but of
course there's the headcount

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.330
and so it's amazing to
see all these people,

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.220
everyone working in this space

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.550
and I just wanna say
thank you to the community

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.350
for all the work you've done.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:33.350
It makes us look good. So
thank you very, very much.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.500
Okay, so that's the summary
of the maudlin part.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.373
What I thought I'd do,
instead of giving like a sort

00:05:43.373 --> 00:05:45.150
of retrospective of everything,

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.290
which you heard a much better job

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.180
from Steve Griffin already.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.550
I'd tell you about some of the
recent stuff that we're doing

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.102
because after 20 years I think

00:05:52.102 --> 00:05:54.663
it's still not getting old circuit QED,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.001
we're still finding out all
kinds of exciting new things

00:05:59.001 --> 00:06:03.330
that you can do and you know,
one bit of progress leads

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.680
to another bit of progress

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.490
and you know in particular
we've been continuing

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:11.340
to bust our heads on this
very difficult problem

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:12.960
of quantum error correction.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.450
And I think we might be
actually turning a corner

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.943
and being able to make some real progress.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.220
But it's also kind of fun

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.140
because we've gone back
to basics a little bit

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.230
and we're reinventing even the
way we do our bosonic qubits

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:26.430
with this dual-rail encoding.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:29.670
So I'm gonna tell you about some of that

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.000
and some of the results and
it's actually joint results

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.756
'cause we have a
collaboration between my lab

00:06:35.756 --> 00:06:39.060
and the group of Shruti
and Steve on campus

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.550
as well as the company in science park,

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.580
and there's probably lots
of people in the audience

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:45.990
here from both organizations.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:50.443
So we've already heard a
lot about error correction.

00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:54.450
In principle, the idea is easy
just redundantly encode your

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.730
information one bit

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:01.730
or one quantum coherence in
three separate physical entities

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.500
and then if all you had was bit flips,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.420
you would be able to be protected
to first order from those.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.480
The problem is of course
now you have encoded

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.340
your information in three qubits,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.060
which have all three of
them errors of their own.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.138
And so when you think about trying

00:07:20.138 --> 00:07:22.517
to actually gain from this,

00:07:22.517 --> 00:07:25.470
the ideas you're gonna
be gaining exponentially

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.820
but you're losing as you put
in more of these elements.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.340
So if you think about trying
to reach break even, you start

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.340
with just having some decay
of your information according

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.840
to whatever the lifetime of that is.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.760
And then even if all of the qubits

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.610
are as good as your best one,

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.980
you're still gonna decay
three times faster.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.950
But we're told okay, if we can
actually measure these errors

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.070
and either correct them or stabilize them,

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:51.510
remove the entropy in some way

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.450
or even just take account
of them in the bookkeeping

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:58.773
of the way we interpret the
results of the algorithm.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:01.980
That'll be okay provided we can measure

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.190
sort of quickly enough that
multiple errors don't occur.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.163
But of course we'll still be
losing no matter what we do.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.060
But the goal of course of these
things is to really be able

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.310
to get some significant
quantum error correction gain

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:20.760
and it's harder than just losing a factor

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:21.990
of three in the beginning because

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:25.200
of course qubits don't just
have most of them a single kind

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.231
of error, they have
multiple kinds of errors

00:08:27.231 --> 00:08:30.780
and so you need to have a
larger code than just three.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:32.910
Maybe you need to do the
surface code which is two

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:35.250
dimensional sort of roughly speaking.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:37.770
Shruti probably won't
like this one dimension

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:41.970
for doing bit flips, one
dimension for doing phase flips,

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.920
but we're told it's okay

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.070
because there's a finite threshold.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.280
There's a point here where as
long as we can get the devices

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.000
to be better so that the
error rate is less than about

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.720
a percent or so, then we can start

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.340
to gain from this error correction

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:03.170
and indeed the logical error
probability will be suppressed

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.640
as we increase the distance

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:10.020
or the size of this code in a way which is

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:11.670
exponential in this ratio of

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.590
how well we do pass the threshold.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.560
And it's great because
superconducting circuits

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:19.386
and qubits have advanced
to the point where yeah

00:09:19.386 --> 00:09:23.070
indeed even the two qubit
errors can be better

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.940
than a percent but they're not
a lot better than a percent.

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.370
And so we're still kind of
right on the cusp of this

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.073
and so things are still
pretty challenging.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.840
So nonetheless there's been all kinds of

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.910
amazing demonstrations.

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:43.830
I'm super impressed at the amount

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:46.620
of effort that's gone in
both on the industrial side

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:49.350
but even in some of the
academic labs on scaling these

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:52.380
things up and you know, people have sort

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.230
of successfully demonstrated
a lot of these concepts

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.390
and we're ready to go in some sense.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.745
But here at Yale, our strategy

00:09:59.745 --> 00:10:01.770
has been a little bit different.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.660
Instead of trying to scale everything up

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:05.250
and then hopefully getting
a little bit further

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.700
past the threshold, we thought
we would do the following.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.014
So you know the story I suppose

00:10:11.014 --> 00:10:13.560
to choose a hardware efficient approach,

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:15.150
we're going to use a qubit

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.992
or something to store the
information that has fewer kinds

00:10:17.992 --> 00:10:20.744
of errors and hopefully
slower errors to begin with.

00:10:20.744 --> 00:10:23.730
And we're not gonna
basically use the hardware

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:24.563
to do the whole thing.

00:10:24.563 --> 00:10:25.800
We're still gonna need to use

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.530
of course conventional
error correction codes.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:31.080
But what we want to do
is make a distance three

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.860
or sort of deal with the first round

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.770
of error correction in some built-in way

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.450
using the physics of the devices.

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.720
And if we can really control
the propagation of the errors

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.720
and all of that, then when you
concatenate these things into

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.040
larger codes, you could really
be way past the threshold.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:52.500
The basic idea is well
okay if we're at a sort

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:56.006
of a percent level now, if
we could somehow in hardware

00:10:56.006 --> 00:10:59.700
get just a distance three,
we'd be able to kind

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.120
of have a squaring here of
this physical probability.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.760
And when you stick that
into your higher order code,

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.440
now you'd really be past the sort

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.030
of thresholds for most of these things.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.967
So the question is, is there a way to kind

00:11:11.967 --> 00:11:16.830
of make yourself first order
robust to hopefully as many

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.370
of the errors as possible
so that you only fail

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:21.360
if you have two errors

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.660
and you can sort of take a
typical performance of 1%,

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:28.800
which is similar for
readout, for two qubit gates

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.260
and lots of other initialization

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.660
and go to sort of numbers

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:35.710
that would be orders of magnitude better.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:41.740
And we also had this really
nice talk setting things up

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.920
from Shruti Puri, from whom
we've learned a lot about error

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:51.870
correction saying that not
all errors are created equal.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.420
Note the punctuation in
the title slide, John,

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:56.843
you're gonna keep track,
you're gonna count for me.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.810
But you know there's a sort
of a hierarchy of errors

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:10.290
and some errors are easier
to correct like the erasures.

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.505
You can also benefit if
your Pauli errors are biased

00:12:12.505 --> 00:12:15.420
and you have a sort of
hierarchy of those things

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:18.630
and you really need to avoid leakage okay?

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:21.840
And so that's something we're going to try

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:25.590
and take to heart here
building into the hardware.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:30.090
So the idea here is we want to maximize

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:34.800
the power of the cavities in
circuit QED which have a kind

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.300
of natural noise bias.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.010
So we've been working a lot

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.923
with this flipped paradigm
if you like, of circuit QED.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.610
It's not that the transmon
qubits are the fundamental

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:48.600
primary interest and the
resonators are there for buses

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.580
or to prevent spontaneous emission

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.360
or to do readout,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.912
but actually we want to use
the cavities themselves also

00:12:57.912 --> 00:13:00.720
as information carriers.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:03.580
And the reason that we kind
of thought that was good is

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.960
I mean in fact we started
on the 3D cavities

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.660
to begin doing the
materials investigations

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:12.540
for IRPA in 2009.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.030
And what we thought is by
making some 3D cavities,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.100
we'll get cues of like 10 to the 11 or 12

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.320
and then that'll be the fiducial we use

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:21.210
to measure materials and everything.

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:24.960
We can make them be
queue of 10 to the eight

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.000
and get to several milliseconds.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.190
But still, you know there's mysteries

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:30.780
even in just these 3D cavities

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:34.140
for why they're not better
than they are currently.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.740
But nonetheless they can be
still better than transmon qubit

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:39.720
in terms of the lifetime

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.600
and importantly they also have

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.730
a large noise bias in the sense

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.220
that they have very little
intrinsic dephasing.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.870
It's a linear element after all.

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:50.850
It doesn't have any Josephson junctions.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.790
It has as smaller surface
participation ratio

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.680
as you can manage.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.260
And you know, we can even
see that in experiments

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.730
where we have this full setup.

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:04.350
So this is from the
paper by Serge Rosenblum

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:07.770
and Phil Reinhold, where
they were able to sort

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:09.450
of tune the dispersive coupling

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.880
between the transmon and the cavity.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:12.870
And there's a point here

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:15.270
where they turned off the interaction

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:19.110
so that you're no longer
suffering any dephasing

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.290
from the transmon,

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:24.450
you're still actually
suffering a T1 perhaps.

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:29.450
But anyway, this thing
had a cavity lifetime that

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.860
was not quite a millisecond

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:34.320
but then you see it goes really very close

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.720
to two T1 when you turn things off.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.360
So from this and from
several other experiments,

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.316
we're able to place a bound

00:14:41.316 --> 00:14:45.030
and only really a bound that
the intrinsic dephasing time

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:46.860
of these 3D cavities, for instance,

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:50.010
is at least 10 milliseconds or longer.

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:52.470
So there's a nice leverage there

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:54.930
and that's one of the reasons
why these bosonic codes,

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:59.370
which allow you to correct for
photon loss should be useful,

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:00.770
should be a way to get gain.

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:06.960
Okay, so again, not all
errors are created equal,

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:11.520
and in particular, these erasure
errors are a special kind.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:15.069
What an erasure error means is that maybe

00:15:15.069 --> 00:15:17.220
an error occurs in a qubit

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.680
but you have a unique signature,

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:21.540
there's something you can measure

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.720
that doesn't involve multi
qubit properties or stabilizers.

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:27.270
You have a flag that
goes off that allows you

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:31.290
to detect exactly when and
where a qubit went wrong.

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:32.460
And of course that means

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.460
that your information you have
is much larger, you can sort

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.500
of prevent the propagation of errors

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:40.200
and it means that the thresholds

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:42.060
for example can move much higher.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:46.110
And so this work on
dual-rail and erasure qubits

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:47.820
in superconducting cavities

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:52.671
that we're doing is very much
inspired by the recent work

00:15:52.671 --> 00:15:57.671
that Shruti did with Jeff
Thompson's group at Princeton

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:02.890
in which they looked at
being able to engineer

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:07.500
a neutral atom platform in which most

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:10.080
of the errors could be then detected

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:14.670
and by that process converted
from spontaneous emission

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.220
or other kinds of
mechanisms into something

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:18.900
that would be classified as an erasure.

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:21.780
And this idea of erasure
conversion I think is one

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.810
of the biggest sort of practical advances

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:26.730
in our understanding of how you should do

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:31.251
error correction in recent years.

00:16:31.251 --> 00:16:32.977
There's another big advantage

00:16:32.977 --> 00:16:36.355
of having something which
is erasure dominated

00:16:36.355 --> 00:16:39.540
and that's because in a way
the errors are a little bit

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:43.920
more classical, they seem
like they scale better

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:47.130
or to put it more technically,
a surface code can

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:49.320
for example correct twice as many erasure.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:51.990
Now you don't have to do
the surface code by the way,

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.810
there are of course if you
had predominantly erasures,

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.670
there are other codes that are small

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.050
and pretty efficient that you can use

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:00.303
to correct the erasures directly.

00:17:01.812 --> 00:17:06.812
Okay, so that's led us to
kind of consider this new way

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.520
of doing bosonic error correction.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.560
We're going to use a microwave cavity,

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:15.720
we're going to add some complexity.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.068
We're now going to encode
the qubit in a pair

00:17:18.068 --> 00:17:21.600
of microwave cavities or microwave modes

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.940
that have let's say different spatial

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:26.223
and different frequency,

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:31.260
different spatial modes
and different frequencies.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:34.260
And the logical encoding
is that we're going

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.380
to have an excitation
which is either in Alice,

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:42.210
the left cavity or in
Bob, the right cavity.

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:46.588
And that just gives us a
simple two level system

00:17:46.588 --> 00:17:48.930
which is your qubit space.

00:17:48.930 --> 00:17:53.930
And the reason this is important
is now if the predominant

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:56.100
error that you're having is

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:59.040
of course the excitation, which
could be either on the left

00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:01.620
or on the right disappears,
then you will go

00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.990
to this unique state zero zero.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:06.720
And in the story I'm gonna
show you there's multiple ways

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:09.424
that you can then detect the fact

00:18:09.424 --> 00:18:11.730
that the photon has been lost

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.370
and you can even do that
without detecting whether it was

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:16.470
supposed to be on the left or on the right

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.113
using mid circuit erasures.

00:18:19.947 --> 00:18:23.835
And so there's a syndrome
here, which means you can have

00:18:23.835 --> 00:18:26.040
a way to detect these errors.

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:30.766
Now there's a proposal
paper that came out,

00:18:30.766 --> 00:18:33.930
a paper about the way
in which to do the gates

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:37.920
and then this pre-print
on the archive showing

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:42.920
some of the results on the
basic properties of these qubits

00:18:43.414 --> 00:18:45.183
that I'm gonna describe.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:51.750
So the dual-rail is not new to us,

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:54.840
it's actually a very old idea.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:58.410
Goes back to linear optics
of course and proposal

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.870
by Chuang and Yamamoto

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:04.860
in just a couple of years after Shore.

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:08.430
And there's many examples

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:10.350
of why you would wanna make
something differential.

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:11.640
You have two things and you look

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.170
for the difference between them.

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:17.100
And so there was proposal
for doing this dual-rail

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:19.020
with transmons from Amazon.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:21.450
There's also a pre-print,

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.120
very contemporaneous from the Amazon group

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:26.460
demonstrating a dual-rail using transmons.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.410
And then there are these earlier proposals

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.830
and work from Will Oliver's group as well.

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:36.390
So it's not that it's
necessarily a unique idea,

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:39.900
but I think the idea of doing
it using microwave cavities

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:43.590
and trying to leverage this
further noise structure you have

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:48.420
of low dephasing could prove
to be more advantageous.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.730
So here's the thing, we want

00:19:50.730 --> 00:19:52.616
to simplify our task of error correction.

00:19:52.616 --> 00:19:56.130
Let's have something which
has a really nice structure

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:59.100
to its error processes

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:03.330
and what we you know can do
is use the physics we know

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:06.127
of circuit QED and these
cavities to kind of predict

00:20:06.127 --> 00:20:08.850
what the major types of errors should be

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:12.990
and what their frequency of
occurrence might turn out to be.

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:16.560
So the erasures are gonna
be set by the T1 time,

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:20.130
that's the fastest error
process in the cavities.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:22.410
So you want your dominant error process

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:24.423
to be the best kind to be an erasure.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:29.910
And so that might be on
timescales of a millisecond.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:33.310
If you're gonna do gates on sort of

00:20:34.470 --> 00:20:36.690
frequency scales given by
the dispersive coupling

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:38.670
to a transmon for instance

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:40.590
or on microsecond timescales,

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.780
that means you might have a
sort of 10 to the minus three

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:46.410
probability of an erasure per timestamp

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:47.973
of the future computer.

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:52.470
Dephasing again we said
okay that's gonna be tens

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:54.540
of milliseconds so that'll
be at least an order

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:56.043
of magnitude suppressed.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:00.270
Now what's a bit flip?

00:21:00.270 --> 00:21:03.540
Well because we have these
two cavities at different

00:21:03.540 --> 00:21:08.190
frequencies, essentially
any linear coupling just

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:09.900
renormalize the mode frequencies.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.480
So if you're not doing
some non-linear process,

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:14.820
there's almost no way for
a five gigahertz photon

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:17.340
to move into a six gigahertz cavity.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:19.710
The only thing that it could
do is relax to the ground state

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.600
and maybe get heated back up.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:24.060
And so that process should be sort

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:26.880
of on timescales of minutes.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:29.010
We'd give parts per million
or parts per billion

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:30.960
per kind of characteristic time

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:34.230
and like heating out of
even the two photon space

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:37.530
or whatever leakage, which
is the worst of all errors,

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:40.200
maybe just basically
effectively never happens

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:43.320
or you can, I think we can
have a non-destructive way

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:48.003
of measuring for leakage here
for almost all the processes.

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:51.090
So here's the full architecture.

00:21:51.090 --> 00:21:52.920
You want to have these
two cavities, you need

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.330
to be able to do operations.

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:56.674
So you need a beam splitter
interaction between them

00:21:56.674 --> 00:21:59.100
that's gonna be provided by some

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:01.140
Josephson parametric devices.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:04.410
And then you're gonna have
ancilla transmon which is now

00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:07.019
going to be used to
like load the photons in

00:22:07.019 --> 00:22:09.060
to measure the resulting state

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:11.850
and to give you a interaction between two

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:14.190
of these dual-rail
qubits when you need it.

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:18.630
And the thing that we described
in this concept paper is

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:21.300
that using all the things we've
developed over the last few

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:25.380
years, we believe that
it's possible to detect all

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:28.922
of the dominant errors in
this system, the cavity loss

00:22:28.922 --> 00:22:31.710
and when you're using the
transmon, the dephasing

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:34.110
and the relaxation of the transmon as well

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:36.923
and convert them into low
probabilities of erasures,

00:22:36.923 --> 00:22:39.390
which makes them much easier to deal with

00:22:39.390 --> 00:22:40.950
and that requires you to use this sort

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:42.633
of three level transmon trick.

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:48.570
Okay, so how do you do single qubit gates?

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:51.150
You want to transfer from left to right,

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:53.670
you need a beam splitter interaction.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:58.110
We can do this parametrically using sort

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:00.870
of tools taken from the amplifier wing

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.138
of the circuit QED community

00:23:03.138 --> 00:23:06.180
and applying them to the
high Q cavity domain.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:09.360
And one of the things that's
nice is now essentially your

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:11.730
alarm mower frequency is the
difference in frequencies

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:13.170
between the two cavities

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:15.810
and you go back to sort
of the standard thing

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:20.130
of using amplitude and
phase of your single RF tone

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:22.526
that you adjust or two tones

00:23:22.526 --> 00:23:25.077
if you're doing four wave mixing,

00:23:25.077 --> 00:23:27.750
but it makes the kind of ideas of

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.790
how you control these things much simpler.

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:32.580
Now Shruti said, oh I told her,

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:35.100
or we tell her that
beam splitters are easy.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.870
I felt like I had to defend my guys

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:42.787
and actually you know, we weren't thinking

00:23:42.787 --> 00:23:45.210
of necessarily the dual-rail

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:47.940
and that we needed to have
super awesome beam splitters.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:49.800
We did know that for all sorts

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:52.590
of bosonic error correction
things we would want to do,

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:55.380
we would need better beam splitters.

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:58.110
And so we launched a couple of years ago,

00:23:58.110 --> 00:24:01.175
three or four separate projects
looking at different ways

00:24:01.175 --> 00:24:04.086
of getting better interactions

00:24:04.086 --> 00:24:06.630
and two of them worked pretty well,

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:08.010
actually almost equally well.

00:24:08.010 --> 00:24:10.560
And so we're not sure yet,
you know, which is kind

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:12.690
of the preferred strategy.

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:17.522
The first of these was to use
this snail device pioneered in

00:24:17.522 --> 00:24:20.220
Michelle's group here at Yale

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:23.430
and biasing it to the right flux point

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:25.755
where you have predominantly
three wave mixing

00:24:25.755 --> 00:24:27.603
because we think not necessarily

00:24:27.603 --> 00:24:30.390
that three wave mixing is
better than four wave mixing

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:32.670
our priority, but because
if you reduce the number

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:34.860
of unwanted processes that'll help you

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:36.660
get a cleaner interaction.

00:24:36.660 --> 00:24:40.170
And so we figured out a way
to get sort of DC flux bias

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:44.850
and RF drive into one of these
two mode post cavity kinds

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:47.800
of devices with this thing that
looks like the right flyer.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:53.660
And we could show sort of
parametric oscillations

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:57.985
here between the left and the
right between Alice and Bob

00:24:57.985 --> 00:25:01.230
that look like this and
they look sort of like

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:03.930
a Rabi oscillation but not,

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:08.610
because they're sort of
falling down toward the ground.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:12.270
And that's a good thing,
that's an important sign.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:16.380
What that means here is that
we're doing fast operations

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:20.460
and that predominantly we
are preserving the nice air

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:22.590
structure of the cavities.

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:25.830
So we might be adding a
little bit of extra relaxation

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:28.042
but usually that's very small

00:25:28.042 --> 00:25:31.290
but we're adding even less dephasing.

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:33.450
Dephasing would take
you sort of to something

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:36.810
where the photon is in
one or the two boxes,

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:40.140
but you just don't know where, which one.

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:42.510
But here what's happening is
really the predominant thing is

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:44.040
just that the photon's being lost

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.290
and that means we can
treat it as an erasure.

00:25:47.418 --> 00:25:52.418
The second scheme is using
this parity protection

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:57.450
and sort of flux pumping a squid device,

00:25:57.450 --> 00:25:58.950
which is essentially a transmon

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:01.020
but pumped in a different way.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:05.550
Again, this is not earth
shattering groundbreaking news

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:06.870
for the amplifier community.

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:08.700
It's just us learning to do it in a way

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:12.120
that doesn't spoil the
hundreds of microseconds

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.840
to milliseconds lifetimes
of these devices.

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:19.680
And so again, you can get
very nice swap oscillations

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.200
between things with this device
we could go even a little

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:24.060
faster and get down to swap times

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:25.860
of like a hundred nanoseconds

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:28.260
or beam splitter rates
of a couple of megahertz.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:33.540
And indeed this looks a
bit like a Rabi oscillation

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:34.740
so we can treat it as such

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:36.422
and say let's just see

00:26:36.422 --> 00:26:40.050
what we can do if we change
the amplitude in the phase

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:43.390
of this beam splitter such that we do

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:48.540
randomized benchmarking on
this block sphere formed

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:52.143
by the single excitation
manifold of Alice and Bob.

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:57.390
And what we could see is something

00:26:57.390 --> 00:27:00.990
where we could do actually
thousands of gates.

00:27:00.990 --> 00:27:05.310
The raw gate infidelity here is something

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.863
a little bit better than three nines,

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:13.260
but again we're falling
towards the ground state here.

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:16.110
So we actually can do a post selection.

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:18.060
We can measure which ones

00:27:18.060 --> 00:27:21.390
of these randomized benchmarking
trials didn't suffer

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:25.380
the photon loss and see what
the fidelity is in that case.

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:28.410
And in that case when we
just post select on the fact

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:31.170
that we have to have a
photon in either Alice

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:34.020
or Bob, we're throwing
out about one in 1300

00:27:34.020 --> 00:27:38.910
of the operations, but
it's improving the fidelity

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:40.170
by a significant amount.

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:43.680
Actually the referee said
what's the big deal about a few

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.810
tenths of a 10th of a percent,

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:47.370
it's a factor of four improvements.

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.350
So I'm sure they weren't in this room.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.050
It is a factor of four improvement
in the overall fidelity,

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:54.030
which again is sort of confirming

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:57.420
that we're preserving the noise structure

00:27:57.420 --> 00:27:59.850
where photon loss is the dominant thing.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:02.010
Okay, so why are we excited?

00:28:02.010 --> 00:28:03.240
This is like two orders

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.400
of magnitude almost faster
than we've been able

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:08.820
to go in other bosonic
codes like the binomial code

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:12.450
and also about two orders
of magnitude higher fidelity

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:14.733
for doing single qubit operations.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:21.270
Okay, so now what I wanna do is switch,

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:23.340
by the way I'm sort of indicating

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:25.320
which lab did the experiments

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:27.750
with the little logo in the
upper right hand corner.

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:28.950
So now I wanna switch

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:33.600
and tell you about using some very

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:36.360
precise measurements with
these dual-rails to try

00:28:36.360 --> 00:28:38.640
and confirm more about
this error hierarchy

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.830
and be more specific about the phase flip

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:43.130
and bit flip rates and
all that sort of stuff.

00:28:44.005 --> 00:28:46.830
So what we're going to do here is an end

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:51.520
of the line projective
measurement of the dual-rail qubit

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:53.610
and we're going to be able

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:56.250
to build into it this erasure detection.

00:28:56.250 --> 00:28:58.410
So just as a reminder, right,

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:02.610
so you can do pretty good
readout of transmons these days.

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:05.430
You can read the ground state very well.

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:08.850
Typically the fidelity
is limited by the fact

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:11.940
that a readout takes
some hundred nanoseconds

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:13.470
to a microsecond

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:17.612
and the T1 time then gives
you about a percent or so

00:29:17.612 --> 00:29:21.780
or maybe a few tenths
of a percent probability

00:29:21.780 --> 00:29:23.130
that you've actually relaxed

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:24.900
during the course of the measurement.

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:27.900
The real problem here is
that a single error like a T1

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:30.480
during the measurement or a misassignment

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:34.800
of which blob you have in
your histogram leads to

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:36.660
an incorrect conclusion about the state

00:29:36.660 --> 00:29:37.803
of the qubit going in.

00:29:38.773 --> 00:29:41.460
Now in the dual-rail, there's
something really nice.

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:44.190
Basically all you ever have
to do in state preparation

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:46.530
and measurement is deal with fox states.

00:29:46.530 --> 00:29:49.875
You need to make N
equals one in your cavity

00:29:49.875 --> 00:29:53.230
and you need to measure N
equals zero or N equals one

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:55.890
and the dispersive interaction

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.440
with a transmon is just
built for that, right?

00:29:58.440 --> 00:30:02.010
There may be a phase dephasing
back action when you do a

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:04.680
measurement of the photon
number with a transmon,

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.920
but it just doesn't matter if
you're doing a preparation in

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.990
one of the logical states or
if you're doing a measurement

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:11.880
in one of the logical states.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:15.900
And so what you can do here
is you can have a transmon

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.600
for instance on both Alice and Bob

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:20.340
and you can make a measurement,

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:22.830
where you map whether there's zero

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:24.840
or one photons onto ground

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:27.660
or excited state of the
transmon ancilla that's attached

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:29.340
to those cavities.

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:32.340
And then you perform a
measurement of each transmon.

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:33.960
You can do that in parallel

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:36.300
and you get two bits of information

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:37.900
and those two bits of information

00:30:37.900 --> 00:30:40.770
are gonna tell you whether
you're in 0 1, 1 0,

00:30:40.770 --> 00:30:42.510
which logical state you're in,

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.300
but they can also give you
extra information, right?

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.870
They will oops, they will
also tell you for example

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.630
whether you get a zero zero

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:52.950
and then you can throw those out,

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:55.020
so you're getting extra information.

00:30:55.020 --> 00:30:56.220
But also importantly,

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:59.520
you're getting something
which is now first order

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:03.870
insensitive to a single
error in this process

00:31:03.870 --> 00:31:08.010
because you have to misassign
both of the cavities

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:09.750
to the wrong photon number in order

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.483
to actually get confused
about the logical state.

00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:16.380
Now maybe if you miss
a photon n equals one,

00:31:16.380 --> 00:31:19.710
call it n equals zero, you
think you're in zero zero

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:20.940
and there was still a photon around;

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:23.490
that's just some extra erasure
probability, as long as

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:25.650
that's small enough, that might be okay.

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:27.120
Here it's pretty big.

00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:30.330
But we've been intentionally
trying to optimize

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:33.030
for the best discrimination
between the logical states

00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.730
to use this as a sort of better microscope

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:37.290
to look at the coherence.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:39.060
And so here are some
of the results that are

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:41.640
in this preprint.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:46.200
There's sort of preparing
the zero and one.

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:48.450
And by the way the preparation
can be done redundantly

00:31:48.450 --> 00:31:50.700
because you can prepare N equals one

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:52.560
and then use the transmon to check

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:55.110
and only go forward when both
cavities have been correctly

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:57.910
assigned to zero or one,
whatever you wanted them to be.

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:01.350
We have a model that takes
into account all the coherences

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:04.170
of the system and seems
to do a pretty good job

00:32:04.170 --> 00:32:05.700
of predicting things.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:09.480
And so when you try to
prepare, let's say one

00:32:09.480 --> 00:32:11.700
or the other of the two logical states,

00:32:11.700 --> 00:32:15.120
there's some probability
that you either actually

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:19.290
or in falsely get a think

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:20.610
that you've had an erasure.

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:22.560
But if you're in the code space,

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:26.280
it seems like you're getting
it correct at the couple

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.329
of parts in 10 to the four.

00:32:28.329 --> 00:32:31.930
So you only misassigned your
logical state sort of 10

00:32:31.930 --> 00:32:33.993
or 20 times out of a hundred thousand,

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:39.210
and that's quite a lot
better than the typical

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:40.533
readouts that one can do.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:47.280
Okay so as I said, now this means

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:49.800
that we can separate out the
different error mechanisms

00:32:49.800 --> 00:32:52.350
within this dual-rail and we can try

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:54.358
and look at whether it's
gonna actually fulfill

00:32:54.358 --> 00:32:58.433
these predictions about the
hierarchy of the error rates

00:32:58.433 --> 00:33:03.090
and the future performance you might get.

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:06.723
So here let's create a super position.

00:33:07.560 --> 00:33:09.900
We do that by loading a
photon into one cavity,

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:13.530
doing a pi over 2 50-50 beam splitter

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:15.420
pulse parametrically,

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:17.970
and then delaying for some amount of time

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:21.337
for particular reasons we're
doing an echo in the middle

00:33:21.337 --> 00:33:24.930
and then doing a pi over
two at the end after that.

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:27.260
And then at the end, you do
this projective measurement

00:33:27.260 --> 00:33:30.120
in the logical basis and
throw away all the things

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:32.250
that are erased.

00:33:32.250 --> 00:33:34.470
The purple here shows you the probability

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:35.760
that you've been erased.

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:39.030
That's just growing at a timescale

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:41.880
of a few hundred microseconds
which corresponds

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:46.620
to the T1 lifetime of the 3D
cavities that are used here.

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:50.910
And what you can see here is
Ramsey fringes or echo fringes

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.456
and they persist out,
you know, past sort of

00:33:53.456 --> 00:33:57.270
the characteristic timescale
associated with the erasure,

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:59.790
because these are keeping only the shots

00:33:59.790 --> 00:34:02.640
that are still in the logical code space.

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:05.440
And so this is Ramsey, this is echo

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:09.300
and the timescales here are pretty long.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:12.540
What I also find amazing about
this is like if we just zoom

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:17.367
in here on the short time,
meaning the first 20 microseconds

00:34:17.367 --> 00:34:19.173
of this dual-rail qubit,

00:34:20.846 --> 00:34:24.693
there's no adjustment
here in the contrast.

00:34:25.590 --> 00:34:27.699
This is taking the raw shots.

00:34:27.699 --> 00:34:32.190
So what you can see here
is that you have resolution

00:34:32.190 --> 00:34:34.920
that allows you to say okay,

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:39.000
we're only getting a few parts
in 10 to the three decrease

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:42.120
of our Ramsey contrast
or our echo contrast

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:43.830
after 10 microseconds.

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:48.270
So the probability of a
phase flip happening is

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:51.390
something like two times
10 to the minus four

00:34:51.390 --> 00:34:53.580
in a microsecond, which is kind

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:55.290
of the timescale we're
gonna be interested in

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:56.793
for doing gates and the like.

00:34:57.660 --> 00:35:01.214
And so that places again,
a bound on the dephasing

00:35:01.214 --> 00:35:04.740
that says we're at least
bigger than a couple

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:06.480
of milliseconds in this case

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:08.580
I think we still haven't
seen the bottom here.

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:11.190
So there can be other exogenous effects

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:13.710
that look like dephasing,

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:17.730
and you know there's also
potentially issues with just

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:19.170
even though the measurement's very good

00:35:19.170 --> 00:35:22.953
involving the measurement
precisely is a good question.

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:28.230
Okay, now I said that even

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:30.570
after you've gotten rid of
the erasures, there's supposed

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:33.390
to be a strong hierarchy between
bit flips and phase flips,

00:35:33.390 --> 00:35:35.850
which again can be
advantageous at least if

00:35:35.850 --> 00:35:38.670
you're just having the qubits
idling and sitting around.

00:35:38.670 --> 00:35:41.520
So we can look at sort
of the bit flip rate

00:35:41.520 --> 00:35:43.938
by either preparing one
of the two logical states

00:35:43.938 --> 00:35:48.600
and then as a function of time looking

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.930
to see the probability
that you stay in the,

00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:55.413
you think you stayed in
the initial logical state.

00:35:56.556 --> 00:36:00.480
And this is actually polluted
here by the fact that

00:36:00.480 --> 00:36:04.020
as the erasure become of order one then

00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:05.910
or start rising here, it's just getting up

00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:09.330
to, sorry, it's over here.

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:12.090
Yeah, erasures getting
up to about of order one.

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:15.480
Now any small probability
that you take the erased state

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:17.040
and assign it to be the wrong logical

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:18.750
state can kind of dominate.

00:36:18.750 --> 00:36:23.400
But again, you see that
these curves are quite flat,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.500
so we can place a bound here

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:29.250
that the bit flip lifetime
is something like, you know,

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:31.833
50 to a hundred milliseconds.

00:36:33.150 --> 00:36:35.950
I mentioned that the bit
flip should be dominated by

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:39.150
relaxation and then heating.

00:36:39.150 --> 00:36:40.170
In a separate experiment,

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:41.610
we can measure the heating rate.

00:36:41.610 --> 00:36:44.730
The excited state thermal
equilibrium population

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:46.320
of these cavities is about
10 to the minus three

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:47.700
or 10 to the minus four.

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:50.310
So the actual bit flip
lifetime we would predict is

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:51.873
something like 15 minutes.

00:36:54.570 --> 00:36:57.660
Okay, enough that it just
doesn't matter, right?

00:36:57.660 --> 00:36:59.060
At least when you're idling.

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:03.243
Okay, so then let me,

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.870
I guess they didn't give
me a strict time limit.

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:10.920
So I'm gonna take a privilege

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:14.460
and go slowly and finish
off with a few things here.

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:18.870
So I mentioned that you want
to not only be able to do

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:22.020
a detection of the photon loss at the end,

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:25.170
but you also want to be able
to check for erasures during

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:27.840
an operation and you
want to do that in a way

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:30.480
that's not also gonna
perturb the logical state.

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:34.230
So if we wanted to measure
a change in photon number

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:37.830
or a photon loss in one of
the usual bosonic codes,

00:37:37.830 --> 00:37:41.550
what we would do is
monitor the cavity parity.

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:44.190
Here to detect whether
there's a photon lost

00:37:44.190 --> 00:37:45.630
from either Alice or Bob,

00:37:45.630 --> 00:37:49.770
what we need to do is measure
the joint photon number parody

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:53.100
or we want to know whether
the system is in zero zero.

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:55.080
And so there's a way to do

00:37:55.080 --> 00:37:57.660
that even if you only
have a transmon attached

00:37:57.660 --> 00:37:59.880
to one of the two cavities.

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:03.870
And the reason for that is
when we drive a beam splitter,

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:06.330
what we're doing is swapping
the photon back and forth.

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:09.180
So if you like the transmon
is sensing both the photons

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:12.300
that started in Alice and the
photons that started in Bob

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:15.056
or it's effectively making these two

00:38:15.056 --> 00:38:19.203
distinct modes look
indistinguishable okay?

00:38:20.147 --> 00:38:23.520
And so I'll show you some

00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:25.890
very recent results from de Graaf

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:27.873
and Sophia Xue who's in the audience.

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:30.900
Stein I think is out with something.

00:38:30.900 --> 00:38:35.640
But what I'm gonna show you here is joint

00:38:35.640 --> 00:38:37.620
photon number splitting.

00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:42.570
So what they do is basically we just can

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:45.630
look at the spectrum of the
transmon here as a function

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:48.420
of frequency and the sort of probability

00:38:48.420 --> 00:38:50.280
that you get excited.

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:53.730
And so just do spectroscopy
of the transmon, whoops,

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:58.140
while you change the beam splitter rate.

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:00.990
And to start with, if we
just are in the vacuum,

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:04.294
you know there's just one
peak, this is not surprising,

00:39:04.294 --> 00:39:09.150
but if you start with one
photon in the system somewhere

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:12.513
and you begin driving the
beam splitter, what you see is

00:39:12.513 --> 00:39:17.130
that here there are sort of
two peaks that are offset,

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:19.950
and as you drive the beam splitter harder

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:22.740
and harder, the states kind of evolve.

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:24.270
As I said, you're making things kind

00:39:24.270 --> 00:39:27.150
of go from originally left and right

00:39:27.150 --> 00:39:28.980
and you're turning these on,
you're getting these sort

00:39:28.980 --> 00:39:31.590
of symmetric and
anti-symmetric combinations.

00:39:31.590 --> 00:39:35.490
And so now the plus and the
minus state or what was left

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:39.000
and right are no longer distinguishable.

00:39:39.000 --> 00:39:39.833
It's probably not the best,

00:39:39.833 --> 00:39:41.040
this is the first time
I'm explaining this.

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:43.440
So it's probably not the best way.

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:46.920
But what you can also see here
in the spectrum is this faint

00:39:46.920 --> 00:39:51.420
line that comes from the
residual zero zero population.

00:39:51.420 --> 00:39:54.600
That's the peak at which the
transmon would be excited

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:57.510
if you had no photons in the system.

00:39:57.510 --> 00:40:01.290
And so to do a check
for erasures on the fly,

00:40:01.290 --> 00:40:04.477
what you do is just a spectroscopic pulse

00:40:04.477 --> 00:40:08.040
about that frequency, which
takes a time of order kai

00:40:08.040 --> 00:40:10.140
or kai over two actually,

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.720
because you're only sensing
the photons half the time.

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:15.780
And so we'll drive with
a beam splitter here,

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:20.130
which is on the order of
kai, the dispersive coupling

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:22.470
and look at what results.

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:26.880
And so here is sort of performing a pairs

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.160
of these erasure checks.

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:32.670
If we start with a superposition,

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:34.860
you can see the following kinds of things.

00:40:34.860 --> 00:40:38.160
So there's some probability that per check

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:40.710
you have an erasure actually occurring,

00:40:40.710 --> 00:40:41.970
that's the green line.

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:44.010
There's some increased probability

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:45.750
of falsely declaring an erasure.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:48.300
So that's something like 4% per check.

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:51.086
But then if you look at the decay

00:40:51.086 --> 00:40:53.940
of the Z logical information,
it's pretty slow.

00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:55.290
In fact it actually seems

00:40:55.290 --> 00:40:57.960
to improve the contrast of the Ramsey.

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:00.150
So we can infer from
that that there's sort

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:03.842
of a few tenths percent probability
of inducing a phase flip

00:41:03.842 --> 00:41:05.970
per one of these erasure checks.

00:41:05.970 --> 00:41:07.470
And you can also do the same thing

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:11.250
to look at the bit flip rate per check.

00:41:11.250 --> 00:41:15.360
This doesn't come because of
heating or something like that,

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:17.330
but because you're swapping many times

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:19.380
and you need to make sure you've done

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:20.970
the swaps precisely enough.

00:41:20.970 --> 00:41:25.320
So a little bit the phase

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:26.820
of the beam splitter or the precision

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:28.380
of the beam splitter turns into something

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:30.450
that looks like bit flips.

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:34.290
Okay so this kind of thing is
the primitive that you need

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:36.780
for doing two qubit gates.

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:41.490
And so again, in these concept papers

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:43.350
we describe a way of doing the gates.

00:41:43.350 --> 00:41:44.450
What you need to do if you want

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:47.070
to do an entangling operation between two

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:49.137
of these dual-rails is engineer some kind

00:41:49.137 --> 00:41:51.780
of an interaction between them.

00:41:51.780 --> 00:41:55.710
And so if the two individual
dual-rails here share

00:41:55.710 --> 00:41:58.560
a beam splitter and you
drive that beam splitter,

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:02.550
you can effectively measure
the joint parody of these two,

00:42:02.550 --> 00:42:05.790
which will depend on not
whether you're erased

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:08.850
but on which logical states
the two qubits were in.

00:42:08.850 --> 00:42:11.640
And you can make this kind
of exponentiation gadget,

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:14.155
where the transmon will
end up in the ground state

00:42:14.155 --> 00:42:18.180
at the end provided you
did everything right

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:23.180
and you impart a ZZ phase
on this pair of qubits.

00:42:23.850 --> 00:42:26.400
And of course the
transmon now is being used

00:42:26.400 --> 00:42:28.440
to give you the non-linearity to sort

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:30.090
of engineer this curve.

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:33.600
The downside is you're suffering
from transmon decoherence,

00:42:33.600 --> 00:42:35.580
but with the three level transmon

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:39.690
by detecting the transmon
at the end, both relaxation

00:42:39.690 --> 00:42:43.770
and dephasing can be found
and turned into erasures.

00:42:43.770 --> 00:42:48.750
And so we get a sort of a
flag just like you would want,

00:42:48.750 --> 00:42:50.700
not only of when a qubit's been erased,

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:52.950
but when a gate goes wrong

00:42:52.950 --> 00:42:56.010
and we can classify that
as well as an erasure,

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:58.610
although it'd be an erasure
now on a pair of qubits.

00:43:00.120 --> 00:43:04.620
So if one just puts in kind
of typical coherence times

00:43:04.620 --> 00:43:06.600
and typical dispersive couplings,

00:43:06.600 --> 00:43:10.080
that means this gate will take
on the order of a microsecond

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:12.387
or two and the erasure errors

00:43:12.387 --> 00:43:14.220
should be on the percent level.

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:17.970
That's kind of like the
standard problem everyone has.

00:43:17.970 --> 00:43:20.730
But the residual Pauli errors come from

00:43:20.730 --> 00:43:22.440
sort of second order things

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:25.170
or the intrinsic dephasing
rate of the cavity.

00:43:25.170 --> 00:43:27.450
And we think those might
even be on the order

00:43:27.450 --> 00:43:29.190
of 10 to the minus four.

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:31.650
And so now you don't want
to talk about the fidelity,

00:43:31.650 --> 00:43:34.590
you have to talk about
multiple kinds of fidelities.

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:37.020
What's the probability of
this erasure error occurring,

00:43:37.020 --> 00:43:39.158
which is easier to deal
with than the probability

00:43:39.158 --> 00:43:41.340
of a Pauli error occurring.

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:44.340
And again, these erasure errors,

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:47.160
they can be corrected more rapidly

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:49.140
and they have a higher threshold.

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:53.460
And so together with Shruti's team,

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:58.460
we've done some simulations
for this system by the way.

00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:00.690
That means that if you have,

00:44:00.690 --> 00:44:02.490
maybe I should go back for one sec.

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:06.870
Having a factor of a hundred
between these is kind of nice

00:44:06.870 --> 00:44:10.650
because it means that
essentially you are gaining even

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:13.470
with your sort of factor of five or 10

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:16.170
past the erasure threshold,

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:18.510
something like beating the
traditional surface code

00:44:18.510 --> 00:44:21.030
threshold by a factor of a hundred.

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:23.981
So you'd sort of like to have these things

00:44:23.981 --> 00:44:26.133
spaced out in this nice way.

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:30.084
So if we do some simulations with kind

00:44:30.084 --> 00:44:33.090
of typical coherence properties

00:44:33.090 --> 00:44:36.420
and you can get erasure rates
on the order of a percent,

00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:39.030
or I mean maybe a little
bit less than a percent

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:41.340
and you really can get Pauli
errors on the order of 10

00:44:41.340 --> 00:44:44.400
to the minus four, even though
you're post-selecting on all

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:46.946
the gates that went wrong, the
surface code can correct for

00:44:46.946 --> 00:44:50.070
that and give you something
which in principle should have

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:54.090
an error correction gain of
something like one to two orders

00:44:54.090 --> 00:44:55.800
of magnitude per layer.

00:44:55.800 --> 00:44:56.970
So that feels like something

00:44:56.970 --> 00:44:59.880
that's actually worth building
into a code and scaling up.

00:44:59.880 --> 00:45:03.330
So the sort of lesson here,
if you didn't take away any

00:45:03.330 --> 00:45:06.060
of the details is that there's a cheat,

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:07.920
error detection is kind of a way

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:10.500
to bootstrap yourself potentially towards

00:45:10.500 --> 00:45:12.630
full error correction.

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:15.150
So that's I think quite amazing

00:45:15.150 --> 00:45:18.600
and something I learned again
from from Shruti and Jeff.

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:21.000
So can you make this
thing into a surface code?

00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:21.960
Yes you could.

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:23.490
It's not a small parts count,

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:26.790
so that might not be an RSL job,

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:31.110
but you only need actually
coordination three

00:45:31.110 --> 00:45:33.600
between your individual
cavities to give you sort

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:37.650
of a 2D grid of these dual-rails.

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:41.550
And you also don't need
a transmon on every site,

00:45:41.550 --> 00:45:43.950
as Shruti was saying, you
can sprinkle them around.

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:47.190
You might only even need
transmons on your measured qubits

00:45:47.190 --> 00:45:48.960
in the surface code for instance.

00:45:48.960 --> 00:45:51.363
And again, you can also
use other smaller codes.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:58.140
3D cavities are not the only
thing inspired by the kind

00:45:58.140 --> 00:46:00.870
of work you just heard about from Natalie

00:46:00.870 --> 00:46:04.308
and working together with
Natalie and Andrew's team,

00:46:04.308 --> 00:46:07.740
we have replicated this tantalum process

00:46:07.740 --> 00:46:12.660
and in this preprint
from my student Suhas,

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.690
we demonstrated that we
could sort of have a budget

00:46:15.690 --> 00:46:19.170
that predicts why a hybrid
transmon is about 300

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:23.850
microseconds, but also why
a on-chip resonator made

00:46:23.850 --> 00:46:27.205
of tantalum should always
beat the Josephson junction.

00:46:27.205 --> 00:46:31.260
Basically you can have a lower
surface participation ratio,

00:46:31.260 --> 00:46:34.260
so you don't have to
think of linear resonators

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:37.830
as only the 3D things, no
matter what materials you have,

00:46:37.830 --> 00:46:40.110
what form factor you make,
you should always be able

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:41.370
to make a longer T1

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:44.730
and a much longer TFI
for a linear resonator.

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:46.050
So that's maybe the reason why

00:46:46.050 --> 00:46:47.157
you might wanna do these things.

00:46:47.157 --> 00:46:49.763
And so the fact that
these are always longer

00:46:49.763 --> 00:46:52.290
and that this now becomes
a little bit more modular

00:46:52.290 --> 00:46:54.510
and easier to scale sort of looks

00:46:54.510 --> 00:46:56.520
like a very nice thing as well.

00:46:56.520 --> 00:46:59.040
Okay, so thanks for indulging me.

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:03.000
I'm not sure how long I went on, I wanted

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:07.292
to thank the entire
team, so both on campus

00:47:07.292 --> 00:47:11.460
and at QCI and thank very
much Shruti and Steve

00:47:11.460 --> 00:47:15.150
and maybe I will just
leave some conclusions

00:47:15.150 --> 00:47:18.280
and summary here and take any questions.

00:47:18.280 --> 00:47:19.113
Thanks.

00:47:19.113 --> 00:47:22.363
(audience applauding)

00:47:38.490 --> 00:47:39.543
There's a couple.

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:46.200
- So this of course it's a very nice talk

00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:47.550
and this is very exciting,

00:47:47.550 --> 00:47:50.880
this new type of error correction.

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:54.870
In the CAT code paper of Niem and Andre,

00:47:54.870 --> 00:47:57.870
there was a optimization
that had to be done on

00:47:57.870 --> 00:48:00.900
how frequently you do the
measurement and checking.

00:48:00.900 --> 00:48:02.283
How does that work here?

00:48:03.390 --> 00:48:06.870
- Yeah, so I don't know
that I understand all

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:08.853
of the details of that yet.

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:15.450
Let me go back to the mid circuit check.

00:48:15.450 --> 00:48:20.450
So you know, if you're trying
to correct for these erasures

00:48:21.210 --> 00:48:23.073
in a higher level code

00:48:23.073 --> 00:48:25.769
and not just do post
selection, then you have to

00:48:25.769 --> 00:48:28.470
interrogate and perform this check.

00:48:28.470 --> 00:48:30.540
You'd like to do the check
as frequently as you can

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:32.850
and it matters what the
back action is, right?

00:48:32.850 --> 00:48:37.680
And so these sort of bit flip
and phase flip probabilities

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:41.520
per check are the kinds of
things that will enter in

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:43.770
and there's gonna be some
optimal measurement strategy

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:47.520
depending on the T1 of
your cavity and so on.

00:48:47.520 --> 00:48:50.460
But you know, in the sort of CAT code,

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:53.670
you're basically exciting the
transmon a hundred percent,

00:48:53.670 --> 00:48:55.530
the whole time in this spectroscopic thing

00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:57.863
you're just exciting it primarily

00:48:57.863 --> 00:49:00.060
when you've already had an erasure

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:02.640
and so you then don't care
about the kind of back action

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:04.593
that limited the CAT code.

00:49:06.300 --> 00:49:09.420
But in addition, sort of Kai T1 in

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:12.780
that experiment was
something like 20 I think.

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:16.680
And so here effectively
it's getting up towards

00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:19.263
a larger number, like 500 maybe.

00:49:20.490 --> 00:49:22.803
So stay tuned. That's
an interesting question.

00:49:24.510 --> 00:49:26.400
I think that trade off
is universal no matter

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:28.300
what you're doing in error correction.

00:49:39.240 --> 00:49:44.130
- So the very top of Shruti
pyramid was biased erasures,

00:49:44.130 --> 00:49:47.880
and I guess you don't have
any biased erasure here

00:49:47.880 --> 00:49:50.883
because your encoding is
zero one and one zero.

00:49:51.831 --> 00:49:53.520
Is there anything, like are you just

00:49:53.520 --> 00:49:55.020
so far out that it doesn't matter 

00:49:55.020 --> 00:49:57.150
or is there anything to
gain from trying to come up

00:49:57.150 --> 00:49:59.730
with a new coding scheme where
you can have biased erasures?

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:03.690
- Right, so indeed biased
erasures can buy you another,

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:06.453
what is it factor of two
or so in the threshold.

00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:10.590
I don't know of a way
to have biased erasures

00:50:10.590 --> 00:50:13.410
for just the cavity photon loss.

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:15.900
I do think it may be possible
to get biased erasures

00:50:15.900 --> 00:50:18.303
for the gate errors.

00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:23.070
Basically, you can figure
out where the qubits were

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:27.210
based on what you see

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:29.850
after the transmon has caused a problem.

00:50:29.850 --> 00:50:32.130
- [Participant] Well I guess
if you had a logical space

00:50:32.130 --> 00:50:36.030
that was, I don't know,
two zero and one one

00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:40.200
or something silly, wouldn't
that be a biased erasure?

00:50:40.200 --> 00:50:44.223
Because then you would decay
out of the two. Stay it faster.

00:50:46.463 --> 00:50:49.233
- Yes but what you want
is something where,

00:50:52.860 --> 00:50:55.383
yeah, okay, but maybe you're just making,

00:50:56.490 --> 00:50:58.080
you want biased erasures,

00:50:58.080 --> 00:50:59.610
but you don't wanna get biased erasures

00:50:59.610 --> 00:51:01.310
by making one of the rates faster.

00:51:03.060 --> 00:51:05.430
You wanna make one of, you
know, the one of the rates go

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:07.213
to zero right?
- Something to be slower.

00:51:07.213 --> 00:51:10.980
- So that's another aspect in
which I think the dual-rail's

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:14.430
kind of one of these happy surprises.

00:51:14.430 --> 00:51:16.260
You know, you only need one photon.

00:51:16.260 --> 00:51:18.700
So the overhead in terms of the

00:51:19.590 --> 00:51:24.530
how many times kappa is
pretty minimal, so...

00:51:39.232 --> 00:51:42.360
- So this is really cool, cool work.

00:51:42.360 --> 00:51:44.040
Post selection is also the domain

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:47.970
of weird physics, like
anomalous weak values

00:51:47.970 --> 00:51:50.431
and non Hamiltonians.

00:51:50.431 --> 00:51:52.770
Do you get weird effects
from those things and or

00:51:52.770 --> 00:51:57.330
can you use like weak value
amplification to make errors?

00:51:57.330 --> 00:52:00.990
- I haven't thought about
the weak value amplification.

00:52:00.990 --> 00:52:04.200
There is one bug that a dual-rail has

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:05.700
that's sort of non-Hermitian.

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:09.720
So if your cavity lifetimes
are not equivalent,

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:13.300
then if you wait around
long enough you will

00:52:13.300 --> 00:52:15.420
almost certainly be in whichever

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:18.750
of the two cavities had the
slightly longer lifetime.

00:52:18.750 --> 00:52:22.420
It turns out that kind
of evolution though is

00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:24.270
sort of deterministic.

00:52:24.270 --> 00:52:28.530
So if you just make sure that
you have an echo pulse midway

00:52:28.530 --> 00:52:31.920
between when you're using
things, then you spend just

00:52:31.920 --> 00:52:34.230
as much time in each of the logical states

00:52:34.230 --> 00:52:37.590
with the excitation in each
of the physical cavities.

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:40.140
And in fact we see that that can sort

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:42.753
of undo this no jump
back action that you get.

00:52:43.680 --> 00:52:45.600
And you know, there's
other reasons why it's nice

00:52:45.600 --> 00:52:47.880
to have echoes in everybody's thing.

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:49.880
So I think that's not much of a penalty.

00:52:56.070 --> 00:52:57.420
But if you have other ideas about what

00:52:57.420 --> 00:52:58.983
to do, we should talk.

00:53:01.770 --> 00:53:03.630
- Okay if we don't have any more question,

00:53:03.630 --> 00:53:05.533
lets thanks Rob again.

00:53:05.533 --> 00:53:08.783
(audience applauding)

